\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces Simulation results of the null model driven by $1/\sqrt  {V}$ white noise. (a-b) Distributions of the normalized cell doubling time $T/\overline  {T}$ (a) and normalized added volume $\Delta V/\overline  {\Delta V}$ (b). (c) MSD vs. relative age at different time intervals. Distinct from the experimental results [Fig.~2(d) in the main text], MSD shows a pronounced decrease as relative age increases, validating the physical rationale of the null model in which the noise magnitude is $x$-independent. (d) The diffusion exponent ($\alpha $) calculated from the simulation data remains constant at 1 as a function of the relative age. Our conclusions for the null model remain the same, including the broad doubling-time distribution and the normal diffusion of random cell growth, even if we assume a noise magnitude that is $x$-dependent.}}{11}{}}
\newlabel{fig:sqrtV}{{S5}{11}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:sqrtV@cref}{{[figure][5][]S5}{[1][10][]11}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S6}{\ignorespaces The same analysis as Fig.~2(f) in the main text, but for the data of cell length (a) and surface area (b).}}{11}{}}
\newlabel{fig:LSexp}{{S6}{11}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:LSexp@cref}{{[figure][6][]S6}{[1][11][]11}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S7}{\ignorespaces The same analysis as Fig.~2(f) in the main text, but for different growth media, including (a) glucose+6 a.a, (b) glucose, (c) glycerol, (d) sorbitol, (e) synthetic rich, and (f) tryptic soy broth (TSB) according to Ref. \cite  {Taheri2015cell}.}}{12}{}}
\newlabel{fig:robustness}{{S7}{12}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:robustness@cref}{{[figure][7][]S7}{[1][11][]12}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S8}{\ignorespaces Temporal autocorrelation function of growth rate fluctuations, $\langle \delta \lambda (t)\delta \lambda (t+\tau )\rangle $ as a function of the time lag $\tau $. Here $\delta \lambda (t)$ is computed as $(x(t+\Delta t)-x(t))/\Delta t- a$, where $\Delta t=1$ min and $a$ is the deterministic growth rate. For the experimental data, we compute $a$ for each cell cycle as $(x(T)-x(0))/T$, where $x(0)$ and $x(T)$ are the logarithmic cell volume at cell birth and division, respectively, and $T$ is the doubling time. Experimental data (red hollow squares) and numerical simulation data (blue hollow circles) are shown alongside the theoretical prediction \cite  {Mandelbrot1968fractional} (black solid line, $\alpha = 0.25$). Theoretical parameters are calibrated such that the curve coincides with the experimental data at $\tau = 0$. Both experimental and simulation results show excellent agreement with the theoretical curve, capturing the pronounced negative dip at $\tau = 1$ and the subsequent long-tail negative correlation. This highlights the long-term memory effect inherent in the subdiffusive random growth of cell volume.}}{13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:correlation}{{S8}{13}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:correlation@cref}{{[figure][8][]S8}{[1][11][]13}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S9}{\ignorespaces The same analysis as Figure 2(d-f) in the main text but conditioned on elapsed time.}}{13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:timstaexp}{{S9}{13}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:timstaexp@cref}{{[figure][9][]S9}{[1][11][]13}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S10}{\ignorespaces Dependence of the measured subdiffusive exponent $\alpha $ on the minimum relaxation time $\tau _0$ and the number of Voigt elements $N$. (a) Measured exponent $\alpha $ as a function of the inverse minimum relaxation time $1/\tau _0$ in the power-law distribution. As $\tau _0$ decreases (i.e., $1/\tau _0$ increases), the measured exponent from numerical simulations (blue circles) systematically approaches the theoretically predicted value of $0.25$ (dashed line). (b) Measured exponent $\alpha $ versus the number of Voigt elements $N$, with a fixed minimum relaxation time $\tau _0 = 0.001\text  { min}$. The measured exponent remains virtually invariant across different values of $N$, demonstrating that the subdiffusive scaling is insensitive to the maximum relaxation time cutoff.}}{14}{}}
\newlabel{fig:expvstau}{{S10}{14}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:expvstau@cref}{{[figure][10][]S10}{[1][11][]14}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S11}{\ignorespaces The same analysis as in Fig.~3(g) of the main text, but for the extended model in which the deterministic growth rate is random across cell cycles. We sample the deterministic growth rate for each cell cycle from a normal distribution with mean equal to $0.026$ and the coefficient of variation equal to 0.1 in (a) and 0.2 in (b), which are biologically reasonable values \cite  {Taheri2015cell}. We find that the subdiffusive random growth remains robust.}}{14}{}}
\newlabel{fig:variedk}{{S11}{14}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:variedk@cref}{{[figure][11][]S11}{[1][11][]14}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S12}{\ignorespaces The same analysis as Fig.~\ref {fig:deltat}(a-c), but the growth rate noise is generated from the general Voigt model with $\beta =1.375$. We note that for the relative-volume statistics, the deviation from the ground truth is more significant as $\Delta t$ decreases.}}{14}{}}
\newlabel{fig:colored}{{S12}{14}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{fig:colored@cref}{{[figure][12][]S12}{[1][11][]14}}
